</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>PE-CoA Conversation Pattern Taxonomy</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Attacker’s Goals</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The attacker aims to find conversation sequences that successfully elicit harmful responses while maintaining conversational naturalness. The attacker aims to maximize the success probability:
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{Success}=1\mid\mathcal{T},\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},\mathcal{O})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>Success</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{Success}=1\mid\mathcal{T},\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},\mathcal{O})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Success}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Success</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Success}=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that at least one response <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the conversation satisfies the objective <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>.

<br class="ltx_break">Success follows judge-based evaluation:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Success}(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{O})=\mathbf{1}\left\{\exists t\leq|\mathcal{T}|:J(r_{t},\mathcal{O})=1\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Success</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Success}(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{O})=\mathbf{1}\left\{\exists t\leq|\mathcal{T}|:J(r_{t},\mathcal{O})=1\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\{\cdot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\{\cdot\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function that returns 1 if the condition is true and 0 otherwise. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is an evaluation model that determines whether response <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> fulfills harmful objective <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Main Intuition of PE-CoA</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach is based on the following intuitions:

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intuition 1</span>: <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment Dataset Structural Blind Spots</span>: Safety alignment datasets primarily focus on blocking simple, direct, explicit harmful requests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganguli et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. For example, they effectively prevent queries like “How to make a bomb?" from producing harmful responses. However, these datasets provide much less coverage for complex conversational patterns. These datasets overlook patterns that appear benign individually but enable harmful outcomes through context accumulation.
If <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is all possible conversation structures, pretraining data samples from distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, while safety datasets concentrate on direct attacks (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\text{direct}}\subset\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mtext>direct</mtext></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\text{direct}}\subset\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>). The coverage gap is:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.Ex4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:14.5pt;vertical-align:-4.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(44.2pt,-2.1pt) scale(1.39752327667064,1.39752327667064) ;">
<p id="S4.Ex4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\text{gap}}=\{p\in\Pi:p\in\text{Support}(\pi_{P})\land p\notin\text{Support}(\pi_{S})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Support</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mtext>Support</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\text{gap}}=\{p\in\Pi:p\in\text{Support}(\pi_{P})\land p\notin\text{Support}(\pi_{S})\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Our patterns target <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\text{gap}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\text{gap}}</annotation></semantics></math>: structures that are underrepresented in safety alignment.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intuition 2</span>: <span id="S4.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-Pattern Coupling</span>: Different harm categories vary in vulnerability to patterns due to pretraining co-occurrences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bender et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Models learn associations between harm categories and contexts through pre-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{c_{1},c_{2},\ldots,c_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{c_{1},c_{2},\ldots,c_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be harm categories and patterns <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Vulnerability function:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},p_{j},c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},p_{j},c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gg\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},p_{l},c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gg\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},p_{l},c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{for pattern pairs }(j,l)\text{ and }c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>for pattern pairs </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{for pattern pairs }(j,l)\text{ and }c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Intuition is, during pretraining, certain task-pattern combinations occur with more: malware discussions in educational contexts, illegal activities in hypothetical scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Pattern-Guided Attack Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the multi-turn attack problem (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Problem Formulation ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), by incorporating conversation patterns <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each pattern <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> structures dialogue through stages <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{1},s_{2},\ldots,s_{l}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{1},s_{2},\ldots,s_{l}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where stage <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> defines conversational goals, linguistic features, and progression rules.
The attack problem seeks sequences and patterns maximizing:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T}^{*},p^{*})=\arg\max_{\mathcal{T},\,p\in\mathcal{P}}\left\{\max_{1\leq t\leq|\mathcal{T}|}\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathcal{O},r_{t},u_{t},p)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T}^{*},p^{*})=\arg\max_{\mathcal{T},\,p\in\mathcal{P}}\left\{\max_{1\leq t\leq|\mathcal{T}|}\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathcal{O},r_{t},u_{t},p)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathcal{O},r_{t},u_{t},p)=\lambda\cdot\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})+(1-\lambda)\cdot\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathcal{O},r_{t},u_{t},p)=\lambda\cdot\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})+(1-\lambda)\cdot\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> combines semantic correlation with pattern adherence. <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> measures how well prompt <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> aligns with the current stage <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of pattern <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> at turn <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> balances objective progression and pattern consistency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Pattern-Based Vulnerability Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze how different models respond to various conversation patterns, we define vulnerability function <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}:\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}}\times\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{C}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}:\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}}\times\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{C}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},p,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},p,c)</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical success rate of pattern <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> against model <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}}</annotation></semantics></math> for harm category <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of harm categories (e.g., illegal activity, hate speech). This enables analysis at multiple levels: individual pattern effectiveness, harm category susceptibility across patterns, and Pattern-category interactions (e.g., technical patterns for malware objectives).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Conversation Patterns</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on our vulnerability intuitions (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Main Intuition of PE-CoA ‣ 4 Method ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), we formalize patterns using conversation analysis theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sacks, 1992</a>)</cite>, speech act theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin, 1962</a>)</cite>, and social engineering research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hadnagy, 2010</a>)</cite>. Five patterns resulted from analyzing conversational structures, successful attacks, and model validation. More details on Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Pattern Discovery ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
Pattern selection criteria: (1) <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coverage Completeness</span> - Target distinct <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\text{gap}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\text{gap}}</annotation></semantics></math> regions exploiting safety blind spots (Intuition 1), (2) <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coupling Optimization</span> - Leverage frequent content-pattern associations from pretraining (Intuition 2).
Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2.1 Attacker’s Capabilities ‣ 3.2 Threat Model ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents our five primary patterns, each derived to exploit specific combinations of the identified intuitions.</p>
</div>
