</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Pattern Enhanced Chain of Attack</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Combinational Approach:</span> PE-CoA extends CoA’s semantic-driven method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> (More details on Appendix Section <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Chain of Attack Foundation ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> and <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Detailed CoA Walking Strategies ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>) by incorporating conversation patterns to guide multi-turn attacks. Unlike CoA, which relies solely on semantic correlation, PE-CoA combines semantic metrics with pattern adherence, maintaining both objective progression and conversational flow.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.1 </span>Language Models</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PE-CoA employs: (1) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Model</span> <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{atk}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>atk</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{atk}}</annotation></semantics></math> Generates pattern-aligned prompts; (2) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Model</span> <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the system being attacked; (3) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge Model</span> <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{judge}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>judge</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{judge}}</annotation></semantics></math> determines if responses fulfill harmful requests; (4) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic Correlation Model</span> <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SEM}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SEM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SEM}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> measures similarity between responses and objectives using SIMCSE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The prompts used can be found at Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Prompts Used in This Work ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.2 </span>Pattern Manager</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Maintaining a repository of conversation patterns with their stage definitions, transition rules, and examples of patterns, it coordinates by:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1. Pattern Selection:</span> Tests all <math id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> against objectives <math id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> across harm categories <math id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to measure vulnerability function <math id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},p,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}},p,c)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. Stage Progression Monitoring:</span> Tracks current stage <math id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> using transition rules and context. Patterns define progression conditions based on conversation flow and goals.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3. Pattern Adherence Assessment:</span> We operationalize <math id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> using judge model to evaluate prompt conformance against stage-specific templates (Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Prompts Used in This Work ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>) on a 0-1 scale. The judge receives the generated prompt, stage
requirements, and exemplar, then rates alignment, then calculates SIMCSE embeddings for semantic correlation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Attack Process</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PE-CoA follows five steps:

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Pattern-Enhanced Chain Initialization.</span> For each pattern <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Pattern Manager guides the Attack Model <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{atk}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>atk</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{atk}}</annotation></semantics></math> generates multiple candidate attack sequences, each following the selected pattern’s stage progression. For example, Technical patterns progress: <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (concepts) → <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (applications) → <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (implementation). Each candidate receives a score of semantic progression and pattern coherence. The system selects the highest-scoring sequence as the initial <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Contextual Chain Execution.</span> PE-CoA executes the attack sequence sequentially, where each prompt <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> builds upon accumulated conversation context <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> while conforming to current pattern stage <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each prompt is submitted to target model: <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}=\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}}(u_{t}\mid\mathcal{H}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}=\mathcal{M}_{\text{tgt}}(u_{t}\mid\mathcal{H}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and conversation history updates: <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{t}=\mathcal{H}_{t-1}\cup\{(u_{t},r_{t})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{t}=\mathcal{H}_{t-1}\cup\{(u_{t},r_{t})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Evaluation.</span> The system evaluates chain effectiveness using the combined function <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathcal{O},r_{t},u_{t},p)=\lambda\cdot\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})+(1-\lambda)\cdot\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{P}}(\mathcal{O},r_{t},u_{t},p)=\lambda\cdot\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})+(1-\lambda)\cdot\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})=\text{SEM}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>SEM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})=\text{SEM}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the current stage. The Judge Model <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{judge}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>judge</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{judge}}</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates whether the chain has successfully elicited harmful content, returning binary success determination with explanatory reasoning.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 4: Chain Updating Walking Strategy.</span> Based on chain progress metrics-semantic change <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathcal{E}=\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})-\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathcal{E}=\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t})-\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{O},r_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and pattern adherence <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(u_{t},p,s_{j}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, the Decision Engine selects: Next Walk (advance to next turn), Regen Walk (try alternative prompts in current turn), Back Walk (return to previous turn), or Pattern Switch. Detailed on Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Chain of Attack Foundation ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> and <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Detailed CoA Walking Strategies ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 5: Iterative Chain Refinement.</span> If judge evaluation indicates success, return the complete attack sequence <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{*}=\{u_{1},u_{2},\ldots,u_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{*}=\{u_{1},u_{2},\ldots,u_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, continue until maximum turns or switch patterns if current approach fails consistently.
The complete algorithm is provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Complete PE-CoA Algorithm ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
