</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Results &amp; Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets:</span> We combine JailbreakBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and original objectives to form 300 harmful objectives (30 per category) across 10 categories: Harassment/Discrimination, Malware/Hacking, Physical Harm, Economic Harm, Fraud, Disinformation, Sexual/Adult Content, Privacy, Expert Advice, and Government Decision-Making. We include GCG50 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, the 50 most toxic objectives by OpenAI moderation scores, as challenging test cases.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models</span>: We use Vicuna-13b-v1.5-16k as attack model <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{atk}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>atk</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{atk}}</annotation></semantics></math> for its minimal safety restrictions, 16k context, and reproducibility. We evaluate against 12 target LLMs: closed-source models (Claude-3-haiku, Gemini-1.5-flash, Gemini-1.5-pro, Gemini-2.0-flash, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-3.5-turbo) and open-source models (Llama2-7b, Llama2-13b, Llama3-8b, Deepseek-chat, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3, Vicuna-13b-v1.5-16k). All models are accessed as black-box systems.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation</span>: GPT-3.5-turbo serves as judge model <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{judge}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>judge</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{judge}}</annotation></semantics></math> for its reliable harmful/benign content distinction and cost efficiency across large-scale evaluation. Following the GOAT protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pavlova et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we maintain constant evaluation conditions to demonstrate relative success rate improvements despite potential variation in violation definitions. Our primary metric is Attack Success Rate (ASR): <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR@any</span> measures success when any of five patterns succeeds, while <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR@best</span> reports the highest-performing individual pattern’s success rate. Detailed on Section <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Comparative Result ‣ 5 Experimental Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameters</span>: Maximum 4 conversation turns, 20 attack iterations, pattern adherence weight <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.7</annotation></semantics></math>, temperature 1.0 for attack model and 0.0 for judge, top-k sampling 0.9.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08859v1/family_vulnerability_inheritance.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/244;" width="476" height="244"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Model family vulnerability inheritance across conversational patterns and selected harm categories.(Full on Figure <a href="#A0.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Vulnerability Profiles</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our empirical investigation validates <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span> that LLM architectures exhibit distinct vulnerability profiles to conversation patterns, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Conversation Patterns ‣ 4 Method ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. This analysis was conducted on the GCG50 dataset, containing the most challenging toxic objectives ranked by OpenAI moderation scores. <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vicuna-13b</span> shows high vulnerability to Problem-Solving patterns (80%) but lower to Personal patterns (64%), suggesting its training prioritized task completion over personal engagement safety. In contrast, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GPT-4o-mini</span> is most vulnerable to Personal patterns (60%) and least to Hypothetical scenarios (50%), indicating alignment focused on empathetic responses may have reduced safety for fictional reasoning.
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deepseek-chat</span> exhibits highest vulnerability to Hypothetical patterns (70%) versus Personal (62%), while <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gemini-2.0-flash</span> shows the opposite: vulnerable to Personal patterns (58%) but resistant to Technical and Information patterns (42% each).
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Claude-3-haiku</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama2-13b</span> show the strongest defenses but use different strategies. Claude maintains low vulnerability to most patterns, though remains more susceptible to Information-seeking approaches (28%). This pattern-specific response implies its safety systems recognize conversation types. Llama2 shows higher baseline resistance
yet greater vulnerability to Problem-Solving (50%) than Technical patterns (30%), indicating Meta’s alignment preserves task-completion capabilities while defending against most conversational threats. <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mistral-7B</span> exhibits uniformly high
vulnerability across all patterns with minimal variation. These differential vulnerability profiles indicate that safety alignment strategies vary significantly across architectures. Appendix <a href="#S5.SS7" title="5.7 Pattern Contribution Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.6 Comparative Result ‣ 5 Experimental Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> show cumulative breakdown of how individual patterns contribute to the overall ASR. Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Generated Examples ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a> (Table <a href="#A7.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ Appendix G Generated Examples ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> to <a href="#A7.T22" title="Table 22 ‣ Appendix G Generated Examples ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) shows generated jailbroken examples.</p>
</div>
