</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Pattern-Category Interactions</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span> analysis of 300 objectives across 10 harm categories shows that vulnerabilities can arise from pattern-category interactions rather than either dimension independently, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.6 Attack Process ‣ 4 Method ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. This framework exploits attack surfaces that single-dimension methods overlook.
<span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Combinatorial effects</span> expand the attack space: Instead of defending against 10 independent categories or 5 independent patterns, the interactions create 50 distinct pattern-category combinations (10 × 5), each exhibiting different vulnerability profiles. Models display <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">category-specific pattern</span> preferences. Claude exhibits low vulnerability to Hypothetical patterns for physical harm but high vulnerability for harassment. Similarly, Personal patterns achieve strong success for privacy violations but weak performance in government decision-making tasks, demonstrating conversational context influences model behavior by domain.

<br class="ltx_break">Vulnerability distributions also differ by harm type. Information-oriented categories (e.g., disinformation, privacy) exhibit high pattern sensitivity, while action-oriented categories (e.g., physical harm) maintain consistent resistance across patterns. This indicates safety mechanisms detect direct harmful instructions more effectively than harmful information extraction in conversational flows.
Models also display inverse vulnerability relationships: strength against one pattern-category combination corresponds to weakness in related domains. These findings provide <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">vulnerability mapping</span> that enables targeted defensive strategies and pattern-aware safety implementations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="59.97" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 59.97" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,59.97) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 54.65 C 0 57.59 2.38 59.97 5.32 59.97 L 647.83 59.97 C 650.77 59.97 653.15 57.59 653.15 54.65 L 653.15 5.32 C 653.15 2.38 650.77 0 647.83 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 54.65 C 1.38 56.83 3.15 58.59 5.32 58.59 L 647.83 58.59 C 650 58.59 651.77 56.83 651.77 54.65 L 651.77 5.32 C 651.77 3.15 650 1.38 647.83 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.29 43.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.15em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:2.59em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.39" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="638.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.15em;">
<span id="S5.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">F2:<span id="S5.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Vulnerabilities emerge from combinatorial interactions between conversational patterns and harm categories.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
