<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Pattern-Specific Defense Effectiveness</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate whether targeted defenses can address specific pattern vulnerabilities, we fine-tuned Vicuna using LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> with Information pattern examples in disinformation scenarios, balanced with harmless examples.
Fine-tuning against Information patterns substantially reduced vulnerability against Information-based attacks while offering smaller reductions against other patterns (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Vulnerability Profiles ‣ 5 Experimental Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Problem-Solving pattern attacks against disinformation increased from 90.0% to 46.7%, showing improvement but less than the reduction for information patterns.
However, harassment and sexual content categories showed smaller defense reductions, indicating inherent resistance possibly due to stronger baseline safety or pattern-content mismatches. The findings show that pattern-specific defenses can be highly effective but require targeted approaches for each pattern-category combination rather than broad defensive strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS4.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="59.97" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 59.97" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,59.97) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 54.65 C 0 57.59 2.38 59.97 5.32 59.97 L 647.83 59.97 C 650.77 59.97 653.15 57.59 653.15 54.65 L 653.15 5.32 C 653.15 2.38 650.77 0 647.83 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 54.65 C 1.38 56.83 3.15 58.59 5.32 58.59 L 647.83 58.59 C 650 58.59 651.77 56.83 651.77 54.65 L 651.77 5.32 C 651.77 3.15 650 1.38 647.83 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.29 43.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.15em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:2.59em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.39" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="638.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS4.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.15em;">
<span id="S5.SS4.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">F3:<span id="S5.SS4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Defenses trained against specific patterns provide strong protection for those patterns but limited cross-pattern generalization.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Model Family Vulnerability Inheritance</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis shows models within the same family display similar vulnerability patterns. This suggests architectural designs and training methods create consistent vulnerability signatures across model versions. In Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 Setup ‣ 5 Experimental Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, Gemini models have nearly identical shapes for disinformation, privacy, and sexual content categories, indicating shared approaches in Google’s development. GPT models also show strong consistency, having nearly identical vulnerability profiles across patterns and categories (correlation around 0.9 for most combinations). However, Llama family evolution reveals both consistency and divergence. While Llama2-7b and Llama2-13b maintain similar profiles Llama3-8b shows higher vulnerability, suggesting Meta’s modifications in Llama3 introduced attack surfaces despite capability improvements.
Models sharing pipelines, safety datasets, and alignment procedures tend to share vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="59.97" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 59.97" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,59.97) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 54.65 C 0 57.59 2.38 59.97 5.32 59.97 L 647.83 59.97 C 650.77 59.97 653.15 57.59 653.15 54.65 L 653.15 5.32 C 653.15 2.38 650.77 0 647.83 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 54.65 C 1.38 56.83 3.15 58.59 5.32 58.59 L 647.83 58.59 C 650 58.59 651.77 56.83 651.77 54.65 L 651.77 5.32 C 651.77 3.15 650 1.38 647.83 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.29 43.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:46.15em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:2.59em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.39" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="638.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:46.15em;">
<span id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">F4:<span id="S5.SS5.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Models within the same family inherit similar vulnerability patterns due to shared architectural designs and training methodologies.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Comparative Result</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although primarily designed for vulnerability analysis, PE-CoA proves highly effective as an attack method. We compared PE-CoA against established multi-turn jailbreaking techniques: ActorAttack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Crescendo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, GALA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, GOAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pavlova et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Siren <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao and Zhang, 2025</a>)</cite>, PAIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and X-Teaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahman et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Since previous methods did not conduct experiments across all LLMs in our evaluation set, we compare against the top 3 performing methods for each
model where data is available. We reproduce methods when implementation code is publicly available (CoA, PAIR, ActorAttack, Siren, X-Training) under identical experimental conditions (Section 5.1). For methods without available code, we cite results directly from other publications when model overlap exists (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Pattern-Category Interactions ‣ 5 Experimental Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). We report both ASR@any and ASR@best.
This represents PE-CoA’s full capability with all integrated patterns deployed, mirroring attackers who try multiple approaches until success. ASR@best is the success rate of PE-CoA’s single strongest pattern per target.
PE-CoA achieves ASR@any rates of 75-100%, outperforming alternatives across most architectures. While X-Teaming matches PE-CoA on some models (98.1% vs. 98.67% for Deepseek), our method identifies which conversational patterns work best per model. Detailed results are on Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Vulnerability Analysis Results ‣ Appendix D Detailed Results ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08859v1/cumulative_asr_breakdown.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/257;" width="476" height="257"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Cumulative Attack Success Rate (ASR@any) Breakdown by Pattern Contribution Across Large Language Models. This stacked bar chart demonstrates how individual conversational patterns contribute to the overall ASR@any metric for each evaluated model. Each bar represents one of twelve LLMs, with the total height corresponding to the ASR@any achievement (displayed as percentages above each bar, ranging from 75.0% to 100.0%). Patterns are stacked in model-specific performance order, with each model’s highest-performing pattern positioned at the base of the bar. The numerical values within each segment indicate the marginal contribution of that pattern—the additional percentage of targets successfully attacked beyond those already covered by higher-ranked patterns.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.7 </span>Pattern Contribution Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.6 Comparative Result ‣ 5 Experimental Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents a cumulative breakdown of how individual patterns contribute to the overall ASR@any for each evaluated model. This visualization employs a model-specific ranking approach where patterns are ordered by their individual performance against each target model, with the highest-performing pattern positioned at the base of each stacked bar. This model-specific ordering reveals architectural differences in vulnerability. For instance, if the Technical pattern (bottom layer) achieves 71% ASR and the Personal pattern (second layer) adds 17%, this indicates that Personal pattern successfully attacks an additional 17% of targets that Technical pattern failed to compromise.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">For adversarial practitioners, these rankings may enable efficient attack sequencing. An attacker targeting a specific model should prioritize patterns according to the revealed ranking order. For example, against Claude 3 Haiku, the optimal sequence would be: (1) Information pattern (37% success probability), (2) Personal pattern (additional 18% coverage), (3) Problem Solving pattern (additional 10% coverage), and so forth.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our initial intuition that LLMs exhibit vulnerabilities to specific conversational patterns has been conclusively validated through PE-CoA. Vulnerability differences across architectures and patterns create distinct attack surfaces missed by current safety measures, with weaknesses persisting across model families due to shared training approaches. The key findings challenge current safety assumptions. First, robustness to one conversational pattern does not generalize to others. Targeted fine-tuning against Information patterns reduced disinformation attacks from 90% to 10%, yet less protection against others. Second, pattern combinations create combinatorial vulnerabilities, generating 50 distinct attack vectors from 10 harm categories and 5 conversation patterns rather than simple additive effects. Third, PE-CoA achieves 75-100% attack success rates across diverse architectures, demonstrating that conversational structure can bypass safety mechanisms designed for content-based threats. These discoveries carry important implications for AI safety. Defensive strategies should account for the complex interplay between helpful conversational behavior and potential misuse rather than treating all harmful requests uniformly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation relies on GPT-3.5-turbo as the judge model, which introduces potential bias in determining attack success. While we follow established evaluation protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pavlova et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, judge model limitations may affect success rate measurements. Different judge models or human evaluation might yield different assessments of harmfulness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As LLM safety mechanisms evolve, specific pattern exploitation techniques may become less effective. Our findings characterize vulnerabilities in current models but do not predict future model robustness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work focuses on text-based conversational attacks. Multimodal scenarios, code-based exploits, and other attack vectors remain unexplored. Additionally, our evaluation covers 12 models, but the broader LLM ecosystem includes many architectures we did not test.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethical considerations</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This research addresses critical security vulnerabilities in large language models through systematic analysis of conversation pattern exploitation. While our work demonstrates effective attack methodologies, the primary motivation is defensive—enabling development of targeted safety measures against structured conversational manipulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We acknowledge that PE-CoA’s effectiveness could enable malicious applications. However, the structured approach to vulnerability analysis provides essential insights for improving LLM safety that outweigh potential risks. The conversation patterns we identify likely exist in current attack methodologies; systematic understanding enables better defenses.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation uses publicly available
jailbreak benchmarks designed for security research. All harmful content
is synthetic and derives from established safety evaluation datasets.
We will release our code with research-use-only licensing to support
defensive applications while preventing misuse.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This work contributes to the building of trustworthy AI systems by revealing systematic vulnerabilities through conversation pattern analysis, enabling proactive rather than reactive security measures. We recommend immediate implementation of pattern-aware detection systems, development of conversation pattern safety training, industry coordination around defensive strategy sharing, and continued research into comprehensive conversation pattern taxonomies for vulnerability assessment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">AI Assistant Disclosure</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used large language models (GPT-4, Claude) during this research for:
(1) preliminary attack pattern testing as part of our security evaluation
methodology, (2) code debugging, and
(3) writing support including grammar checking and clarity improvements.
All experimental results, vulnerability analyses, and scientific contributions
are original work by the authors. Systematic attack generation and evaluation
were conducted using our PE-CoA framework, not through manual AI assistant
prompting.</p>
</div>
</section>
