<figure id="A0.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08859v1/PE-CoA_intro.svg" id="A0.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/279;" width="429" height="279"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Asymmetric vulnerability: Given an objective in multi-turn scenario, <span id="A0.F7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hypothetical Pattern</span> attack jailbreaks LLM 2, while LLM 1 maintains safety, whereas the <span id="A0.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Information Seeking Pattern</span> jailbreaks LLM 1, while LLM 2 resists. PE-CoA demonstrates that models exhibit distinct vulnerability profiles to
conversational patterns. We introduce five patterns and analyze their
effectiveness in different LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
