<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Performance Comparison with Baseline CoA</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We comprehensively compare PE-CoA against its foundational Chain of Attack (CoA) framework to demonstrate the value of pattern-guided approaches. Our analysis examines overall success rates, pattern-specific effectiveness, cross-model transferability, and convergence efficiency. To ensure fair comparison, we reran the baseline CoA method alongside PE-CoA under identical experimental conditions and evaluation protocols.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Overall Performance Improvement</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ D.1 Vulnerability Analysis Results ‣ Appendix D Detailed Results ‣ Pattern Enhanced Multi-Turn Jailbreaking: Exploiting Structural Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> presents a direct comparison of attack success rates between CoA and PE-CoA@any on the GCG50 dataset across three models.

<br class="ltx_break">PE-CoA demonstrates substantial improvements over the baseline CoA across all evaluated models. These improvements result from PE-CoA’s structured conversation development, which creates natural attack progressions that better evade safety filters. The significant gains on well-aligned models like GPT-3.5 indicate pattern-guided approaches are particularly effective where traditional methods struggle.</p>
</div>
